#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065232} :activity/id #uuid "6b61e9db-9682-5425-ad74-a269bcaceb50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049026 :topic/id #uuid "d49a6c77-a56c-5955-b68f-69307587aa23" :topic/math-academy-id 352} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065341 :tutorial/content "A **perfect square trinomial** is a three-term expression that is the result of squaring a binomial.\n\nFor example, the expression\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + 9\n$$\n\nis a perfect square trinomial because it is the result of squaring the binomial $(x+3)$, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial, we have to figure out what binomial it is the square of. We can often do this by relating the perfect square trinomial to the **square of a sum**:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2+6x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the \"square of a sum formula\". In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{3}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nThis method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. We will learn more general methods in the future." :tutorial/id #uuid "c04b9f03-f29f-5feb-8d13-7de9fc378a34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1596 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c96b380f-2e0a-5243-a862-5219272b92d7" :step/math-academy-id 4226 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064325 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063960 :question/id #uuid "d496f23e-6ef7-5ec2-bb6c-054759a5b033" :question/math-academy-id "e-1327"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373d4ad5-ef39-5f03-bba4-d9be901e2587" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049847}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482579}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Sum"} :step/id #uuid "d98ff5d5-8ca1-5834-941d-13d2afb9da6f" :step/math-academy-id 4227 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065256}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064326 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063961 :question/id #uuid "29cbd59e-9eff-5439-9551-4520f68707b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1328"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "457399ac-1ede-551f-968b-9ef270a67d0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507455}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"} :step/id #uuid "1253a4a8-20c7-58c3-9e8e-893e406761f2" :step/math-academy-id 4230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064327 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063962 :question/id #uuid "2fb03f0f-25c2-57a6-9e33-da837af2f339" :question/math-academy-id "e-1329"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48f257af-1f00-5cf9-89cd-3d44d21482ec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507457}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial with Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "59f4cd9a-a9f4-5779-b601-96d0642330dd" :step/math-academy-id 4232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065345 :tutorial/content "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the **square of a difference**:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}2}  {\\color{blue}a}x  + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\n \n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2-10x+25$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{5}},$ and we get\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n  $$" :tutorial/id #uuid "70396264-e3c3-50d9-94a4-95e8017dbfde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1855 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"} :step/id #uuid "5e99daa1-dbe7-5fe2-bda6-1e302fb1a276" :step/math-academy-id 6169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065234}}] :activity/title "Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064441}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507576} :activity/id #uuid "f69f02b5-d258-52b6-bfe5-2cbd30c0d419" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049026 :topic/id #uuid "d49a6c77-a56c-5955-b68f-69307587aa23" :topic/math-academy-id 352} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca67fe0-cf38-599e-96f0-f21b24a8ed45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2e+9)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506700} {:answer/id #uuid "1e211f2a-503f-53ec-afa6-8cc382355c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e-9)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506739} {:answer/id #uuid "836e756a-76b5-5f29-86b1-a991836e11bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506970} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a4827a-5dc1-5018-8b60-d93186610d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e+9)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507110} {:answer/id #uuid "c91d77da-831a-57b5-a1ac-15897539bebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2e-9)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506739} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8ac7d31-1c83-57d9-9043-68e47336ef2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85b0de0c-7078-58c3-80c6-e70c35748f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-3001" :question/problem "Factor the expression ${e}^{2}-18e+81.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{e}^{2}-2ae+{a}^{2}=(e-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{e}^{2}-18e+81.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2}-18e+{9}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2}-2\\cdot 9e+{9}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(e-9)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2367216d-f79e-5890-b1c7-ed65906fb24f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bfa4c3-af30-5756-82bb-059f1b23c4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506843} {:answer/id #uuid "4850c323-0008-5586-a92c-51d27d9d80e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{25}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506847} {:answer/id #uuid "9af9ba7a-b83f-5100-8ba8-0c8c301f0339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507013} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4ea64c-142a-5719-b240-6ec5e8ce7a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507054} {:answer/id #uuid "b6500cbd-5e5a-59f1-8c25-13b96afe7d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507080}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506843} :answer-field/id #uuid "f20bb066-c188-50d4-908b-5327e0942d6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8c418bae-22aa-5bc3-86d4-82df570c3162" :question/math-academy-id "q-78927" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{5}{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fa30973-6c9b-5f0a-bfce-fb179d74bbe8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31315a0d-2c0e-5f94-a1ff-4b7faeb27a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506794} {:answer/id #uuid "3701d3f2-5410-556e-b0a7-75420b9f36b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506810} {:answer/id #uuid "374322ff-4086-5b83-87ee-c3122f61bd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+3)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506811} {:answer/id #uuid "c0be764b-beb0-5776-8b10-64f11f6978bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507101} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c7dbd4-3ec1-54d0-8413-03bba2ac8751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506794} :answer-field/id #uuid "8adb6eba-9169-5d39-b01b-4fde7ef436d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e023823-cd56-5877-8c6f-e730b2d09ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-78924" :question/problem "${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}=$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "840aef68-9d30-531e-8a4f-9c7f5eaf1292" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22440f7b-035c-540b-b0e2-3dcd53956334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506747} {:answer/id #uuid "24264748-c5f8-54b5-aa97-cacdbd3e54a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506756} {:answer/id #uuid "9798202a-5866-5647-b043-5b3551c6a2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507008} {:answer/id #uuid "baca3216-958b-5e79-b70f-2b61c19055ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507089} {:answer/id #uuid "c1195dcf-977f-50d8-932c-4e76e5fdb1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506756} :answer-field/id #uuid "b73f9e06-0243-5c21-9043-a7def06f5f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "14f47d14-d694-51ea-abc7-d932943c0b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-44172" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "893ea4e4-86f8-579b-bce8-6341f6a5776a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a6cd19d-164d-5b97-a602-10c686dd9873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2t-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506985} {:answer/id #uuid "9df797c2-4cab-5309-a7be-3552ea5721d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(t-12)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507017} {:answer/id #uuid "a42dd5ae-f26b-5919-9491-b0ee466749f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2t-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507035} {:answer/id #uuid "cc44d5db-8cdb-56b6-ac5a-21a07d054d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(t+11)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507123} {:answer/id #uuid "e43e106b-fd87-54fd-9560-b587c9f88927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(t-11)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507177} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b1d029e-2f62-5e77-9841-4721fc7760f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f9f643d-cb40-58cc-a000-e0bdfa821c71" :question/math-academy-id "q-2831" :question/problem "Factor ${t}^{2}-22t+121.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2at+{a}^{2}=(t-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-22t+121.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-22t+{11}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2\\cdot 11t+{11}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=11.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-11)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cc3b16d6-8646-5e1f-a04f-001ad7825849" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507539}}] :activity/title "Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506676}]}
